    "lawCode": "Amd §987, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S8054\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to notice of\nunpaid taxes in the county of Monroe\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo increase the number of days after the expiration of the tax\ninterest free period to ninety days for Monroe County to mail their\nunpaid tax notices.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 - amends subdivisions 1 and 2 of section 987 of the Real\nProperty Law to increase the unpaid tax notice mail time-frame from\neighty-five to ninety days for Monroe County.\n\nSection 2 - of the bill provides that this act shall take effect\nimmediately\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nMonroe County is unique in the state of New York in that it allows\nfour installments in its Town and County tax collection. The final\ninstallment has a due date of April 30th. In order to comply with the\ncurrent law, the unpaid notices must be mailed by May 6th. To allow\nfor enough time to prepare the notices for mailing, the county must\nrun its records that identify those with an outstanding balance on May\n2nd. Tax payments for the 4th installment are still arriving in the\nmail for several days following May 2nd, resulting in numerous notices\nbeing mailed to taxpayers who have timely sent their final\ninstallment. In addition, when the due date lands on a weekend, as it\ndid in 2016, the due date is extended to the following business day,\nwhich was the 2nd of May. Unnecessary mailing of unpaid tax notices is\nnot only a waste of taxpayer dollars to generate and mail them, but\nalso spawns a flood of phone calls from residents who had paid their\nfourth installment and are confused, and at times angry, that they\nhave received the notice.  Adding five more days to the window\nrequired for mailing of notices would have a dramatic effect in\nreducing the number of unpaid tax notices being generated.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to state. Savings in production and postage costs will be\nrealized by local taxing authorities as notices will no longer be\nproduced and mailed to residents that mailed their fourth installment\nor partial or full payment close to or on the due date. Phone call\nvolume will also be substantially reduced since residents will no\nlonger be confused as a result of receiving a notice after they have\nmade payment. This will allow Town and County staff to address other\ncritical services to their constituents.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
